{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was a prominent figure in the Crusader states in the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He was born in 1195 and reigned as count from 1210 until his death in 1225. Raymond III was the son of Raymond II, Count of Tripoli, and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His family was one of the most powerful in the Crusader states, and his mother was a member of the royal family of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by significant political and military challenges. He had to navigate the complex power dynamics of the Crusader states, which often involved conflicts with neighboring Muslim powers as well as rivalries among the Crusader leaders. The most notable of these conflicts was the Ayyubid invasion of the Crusader states. This invasion was led by the powerful Muslim leader Saladin and posed a severe threat to the survival of the Crusader states. Raymond III was forced to defend his territory against these attacks, which often involved intense and prolonged military campaigns.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III's reign also saw the diminishing influence of the Crusader states. The Crusader leaders often quarreled among themselves and were unable to form a united front against their enemies. This internal strife weakened the Crusader states and made them vulnerable to external threats. Raymond III was one of the few leaders who attempted to unite the Crusader states against their enemies, but his efforts often met with resistance from other leaders. Despite these challenges, he was able to maintain control over Tripoli and its surrounding territories for much of his reign.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III's death in 1225 marked the end of an era. He died without heirs, and the county of Tripoli was inherited by his sister, Melisende. His reign was a tumultuous period in the history of the Crusader states, marked by both successes and failures. His efforts to defend his territory and unite the Crusader states against their enemies were significant, but he was ultimately unable to prevent the decline of the Crusader states. Raymond III's legacy is complex, reflecting the difficulties and challenges of the time in which he lived.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.509621500968933}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00029452278977259994}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0004183370911050588}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.11129581183195114}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.017390621826052666}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.9934315282152966e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -8.105902816168964e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.266281055402942e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.38046666979789734}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.5553903579711914}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.10079587996006012}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.9709744453430176}, {"token": " figure", "logprob": -0.14915910363197327}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.17333577573299408}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.04832588881254196}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.43593308329582214}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.061672329902648926}, {"token": " states", "logprob": -0.29662710428237915}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.1287264823913574}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.009426843374967575}, {"token": " late", "logprob": -1.6770122051239014}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.016431959345936775}, {"token": "12", "logprob": -0.06444089114665985}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -0.0012528197839856148}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.8323571681976318}, {"token": " early", "logprob": -0.0010131231974810362}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.728519782773219e-05}, {"token": "13", "logprob": -4.184158387943171e-05}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -0.0008424547268077731}, {"token": " centuries", "logprob": -0.027668854221701622}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0638582